✅ 1. What is Garbage Collection (GC) in Java?

Garbage Collection (GC) is an automatic memory management feature in Java that:

✔ Frees memory by removing objects that are no longer reachable

✔ Prevents memory leaks

✔ Prevents OutOfMemoryError

✔ Maintains long-running JVM stability

Java developers don’t manually free memory (like in C/C++ with free()), JVM does this job.

---

⚙️ 2. JVM Memory Model (Very Important for GC)

Java memory areas:

A. Heap (GC TARGET)

All objects created using new keyword are stored here.

Divided into:


Young Generation
   ├── Eden
   ├── Survivor 0 (S0)
   └── Survivor 1 (S1)

Old Generation (Tenured)

Metaspace (Class Metadata)


GC focuses on Heap only.

---

🔍 3. Young Generation GC = Minor GC

Happens frequently

Very fast

Collects objects that live for a short time

Minor GC Steps

1. Objects created → go to Eden
2. When Eden fills → Minor GC runs
3. Live objects moved → Survivor Space S0
4. Next Minor GC moves S0 → S1 (flip)
5. After certain age (default 15), object moves → Old Generation

Example: Object Aging


age 0 → Eden
age 1 → S0
age 2 → S1
age 3 → S0
...
age 15 → Old Gen


💡 Minor GC does not pause the whole JVM long.

---

🔥 4. Old Generation GC = Major / Full GC

Triggers when:

 Old Gen almost full
 System.gc() is called
 Memory pressure increases

Major GC involves:

 Stop-the-world pause
 Much slower than Minor GC
 Can cause CPU spikes and latency

---

🔄 5. Types of GC Algorithms in Java

| GC Type                     | Target          | Advantages         | When to Use              |
| --------------------------- | --------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------ |
| Serial GC                   | Single-thread   | Simple             | Small apps               |
| Parallel GC                 | Multi-thread    | Max throughput     | CPU rich, batch systems  |
| CMS (Deprecated)            | Low pause time  | Concurrent         | Legacy applications      |
| G1 GC (Default for Java 9+) | Regions         | Predictable pauses | Most modern systems      |
| ZGC                         | Ultra-low pause | HUGE Heaps         | High-scale microservices |
| Shenandoah                  | Similar to ZGC  |                    |                          |
| Epsilon GC                  | No GC           | Testing only       |                          |

---

🚀 6. Deep Dive: Modern Garbage Collectors

A. G1 Garbage Collector (Most Important)

G1 = Garbage First

Heap divided into many small regions (1–32 MB)
Instead of Young/Old fixed size, G1 picks regions with highest garbage first.

✨ Benefits

 Predictable pause time
 Handles large heaps
 Great for microservices

How G1 Works

1. Identifies garbage-heavy regions
2. Performs evacuation
3. Compacts memory
4. Avoids huge pauses

---

B. ZGC – Ultra Low Pause

Pause time < 1 ms
Designed for heaps 100GB – 16 TB.

Uses:

 Load barriers
 Colored pointers

---

🧠 7. How GC Knows an Object is Garbage? (Reachability Analysis)

GC uses reachability from GC roots.

GC Roots

 Local variables in stack frames
 Static variables
 JNI references
 Thread objects
 Active classes

If an object is not reachable → Garabage

Example:

java
public void test() {
    Person p = new Person();  // reachable
    p = null;                 // unreachable → GC candidate
}


